r the meryl  streep of dogs coco had humble  beginnings she started off as a shelter  dog and made it to the big time with no  training amber referred to her as  a little bit of a hot mess but in a  sweet way coco wasn't intended to have  star billing they truly didn't mean for  her to be in as much of the movie as  they had her but her presence was just  so fun that she kept being asked back  number nine unlike the doggo the horse  was a natural dakota beavers may look  like a natural rider in the film but  trust us he only learned for filming  during that four week boot camp he was  riding at every possible minute and it  didn't stop there on his days off from  filming he was back on the horse and  riding again  number ten tall guys can do parkour too  the six foot nine actor who played the  predator lost 25 pounds to play the role  he also had an aggressive workout  routine he said that they wanted a lean  dynamic more animal predator  traditionally he's more of a muscular  guy he trained in parkour for two months  saying i'm six foot nine not many six  foot nine guys do parkour he also  trained in martial arts for two months  specifically his neck he teamed up with  a company called iron neck and  strengthened it for two months because  of the heavy predator head it was about  13 pounds or something which weighed  well over 10 pounds on top of all the  acting he did all the stunts and the  motion capture too number 11. why did  school take 14 years if you can learn so  many skills in a month four weeks in  calgary were dedicated to preparing  actors for their roles they did stunt  choreography and weapon training we did  the archery and spears and knives and  the tomahawk amber learned every single  sequence in its entirety because she  wanted to do everything herself saying  so i got to jump off the tree i got to  get thrown off the predator i got to  fight him despite the training amber  admitted there's nothing that really  prepares you for a week in a mud pit you  know number 12 wear a life jacket  whenever possible it may save your life  one location that really made amber  uncomfortable was the river for good  reason she said i grew up around water  but i also grew up knowing how dangerous  it is but it's okay she managed  admitting i had anxiety building on top  of anxiety on top of anxiety but once i  got in it was so fun while she did get  used to the river for the most part  there was one bit that no one could get  used to and that was the temperature  because even though it was summertime it  was glacial runoff water amber said we  were there for like five days so for  five days i would wake up and be like  ice bath number 13 not every great idea  makes it the director is a bit  disappointed that one scene he really  wanted never came to fruition a treetop  chase he said i was for whatever reason  really obsessed with having the fights  be more vertical and seeing the predator  leaping through the trees too bad that  sounds like fun maybe they'll get there  in a sequel or another prequel  number 14. coco isn't the only one who  had her film debut prey was dakota  beaver's first movie he'd auditioned for  something that fell through but his name  was still on file when he got an email  he was under the impression that this  was for a small part he sent in a self  tape then a zoom audition then another  one then he was flown out to la for a  screen test with amber only then did he  realize it wasn't just a small part all  of this flying was pretty wild for him  because he'd never really been out of  the country too much before and never by  plane so flying during covent for  filming was eerily exciting because the  airport felt like a ghost town  number 15. how do you make a prototype  predator just as scary probably the  biggest struggle anyone had was  reimagining the predator in a way that  would keep it fresh and creepy while  still honoring the original design they  really had to do something else to  better fit this story  but also the predator has a familiar  face to people now so in order to keep  the scare factor up they needed that  redesign to keep people on their toes  it's like kids being scared of santa the  first time they sit on his lap compared  to five years later okay maybe it's not  like that  you know that hollywood saying never  work with animals or children but if you  have to pick one pick a shelter dog and  if you have to pick two pick a horse as  well were you impressed by the design of  the feral predator let us know in the  comments and thanks for hanging out with  us here at the things
 

